:: Activity Toilet [activity] {"position":"775,1800","size":"100,100"}
/* A trip to the toilet (Bathroom: "take a minute and be done with it"). Spends the rest of the time slot, no effects.
   Mostly me on my phone. Stage 0: rejections, the diner, $mom and her boss. Stage 1: the company chat, clients, the office feed. */
<<silently>>
<<set _pool = ["bottles"]>>
<<if $company.stage >= 1>>
	<<run _pool.push("bossMail", "calendar")>>
	/* the team chat and the office feed need the staff hired (Too Much Work); the feed also needs a camera hidden in the office (CMD CCTV) */
	<<if setup.questDone("main_tooMuchWork")>><<run _pool.push("teamChat")>><</if>>
	<<if setup.hasCamera("companystage1") && setup.questDone("main_tooMuchWork")>><<run _pool.push("feed")>><</if>>
<<else>>
	<<run _pool.push("rejection", "schedule", "server")>>
	<<if !setup.questDone("mom_sabotage")>><<run _pool.push("boss")>><</if>>
<</if>>
<<set _toiletScene = _pool.random()>>
<</silently>>
Some of my best thinking happens in here. It's not something I'd put on a pitch deck.

<<switch _toiletScene>>
<<case "bottles">>
There are eleven bottles lined up along the edge of the tub. I count them twice. One is mine, one is $mom.callName's, and the other nine belong to $sister.callName, including three that are, as far as I can tell, the same shampoo in different colors. One of them promises "a salon experience." Another one promises "a vacation for your hair."

I read every label. I learn that my $sister.relation's hair has been to more places than I have.

<<case "rejection">>
New mail. "Thanks so much for reaching out!" Exclamation mark. Nobody has ever opened a yes with an exclamation mark. I read the rest anyway: they love the vision, the timing isn't right, they'll keep me in mind for the future, which is a place where nobody has ever been kept in mind for anything.

The guy who wrote it is twenty-four. The fund was founded by a man with the same last name. I close the app and sit there with the phone in my hand, looking at nothing, for a while.

<<case "schedule">>
$manager.callName posted the new schedule in the diner group chat. My name is on the Saturday double again, with a little note next to it in his special font, all caps: DON'T BE LATE, FOUNDER.

Four people put a laughing emoji on it. One of them is the dishwasher, who doesn't speak to me. I tap on the reactions and read the names, one by one, slowly, so they stick.

<<case "server">>
The hosting company would like to remind me, in a friendly tone, that my invoice is fourteen days overdue. They've attached a cartoon of a little cloud looking worried. The cloud and I have a lot in common.

I do the math on the back of my hand with a fingernail. Pay the server and eat properly this week, or pay the server and buy the parts for the next board. I go with the parts. The cloud can stop worrying, at least. One of us should get to.

<<case "boss">>
$mom.callName came home late again yesterday, sat down at the kitchen table without taking her coat off and just stayed there. $boss.callName had made her redo the quarterly report. Three times. Because of a font.

I look him up while I'm sitting here. Fifty-seven, a corner office, two divorces and a profile photo taken on somebody's yacht. Everybody has something that makes them move. Some people are kind enough to post it online.

<<case "teamChat">>
The team chat is busy. $ethan.name has posted a meme about a programmer and a rubber duck. $brian.name has replied with a three-paragraph technical correction of the meme. $darius.name has reacted to both with the same neutral thumbs up, which I'm pretty sure is a data point he's collecting.

I add a thumbs up to all three. Leadership.

<<case "bossMail">>
$boss.callName has sent a mail. "Dear $player.surname, following up on our arrangement," and so on, three paragraphs of perfectly formal English with nothing in them. He signs it "Best regards." From him that reads like a threat, and from me, when I answer, it's going to read like one too.

I start the answer right here, with one thumb. It's polite. It's very polite. I save it as a draft, so I can make it even more polite later.

<<case "calendar">>
$mom.callName has put a meeting in my calendar for tomorrow at twelve-thirty. It's called "LUNCH (please actually eat)." She has marked it as high priority and sent it to herself as well, probably so she can check that I've accepted.

I accept it. Then I move my two o'clock so it won't run into it, because she'll check that too.

<<case "feed">>
I open the office camera feed on my phone. Not for anything. $brian.name is hunched over his bench. $ethan.name has his feet on the desk. $darius.name is standing at the window with a coffee, the way he does when he's thinking.

Nobody's doing anything interesting. They're all doing exactly what they're supposed to be doing, at exactly the pace I'd want, and none of them knows I'm looking. I watch them for a few minutes anyway. It's oddly relaxing.
<</switch>>

I flush and wash my hands. The phone buzzes again before I've dried them.

<<link "Finish" "Bathroom">>
    <<AdvanceTime>>
<</link>>
